.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt VHCI 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vhci
.Nd virtual USB host controller for USB/IP
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver at boot time, add this line to
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vhci_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver presents a USB host controller that has no hardware behind it.
Its root hub's ports are filled in by
.Xr usbip 8 ,
which connects to a USB/IP server, performs the protocol handshake, and
hands the connected socket to the driver.
From then on the driver forwards USB transfers over that socket, and the
remote device appears on the local USB bus as though it were plugged in
here.
Ordinary class drivers attach to it in the usual way.
.Pp
The root hub is a USB 2.0 hub with eight ports, so up to eight remote
devices may be attached at once.
Each occupied port owns one TCP connection and a pair of kernel threads
that carry transfers to and from the server.
.Pp
Control, bulk and interrupt transfers are supported.
Isochronous transfers are not.
.Pp
The driver creates
.Pa /dev/vhci ,
through which
.Xr usbip 8
attaches and detaches devices and lists the ports.
The interface is not intended to be used directly.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
A USB/IP server has already enumerated and addressed the device it
exports, and identifies it by a device id rather than by USB address.
.Nm
therefore answers
.Dv SET_ADDRESS
locally instead of forwarding it, which would otherwise change the
address the server itself is using and break the session.
.Pp
Everything the driver reads after the hand-off arrives over the network
and is parsed in kernel context.
A reply that does not make sense against the transfer it claims to
answer causes the session to be dropped rather than acted on.
.Pp
If the connection is lost, the port is released and the device
disappears as though it had been unplugged.
Transfers outstanding at that moment fail; they do not hang.
.Pp
The USB stack brings the root hub up on its own thread, so the bus is
still coming up when the driver finishes attaching.
Unloading during that window is refused with
.Er EBUSY ;
retrying a moment later succeeds.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr usb 4 ,
.Xr usbconfig 8 ,
.Xr usbip 8
.Pp
The protocol is described at
.Lk https://docs.kernel.org/usb/usbip_protocol.html .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver was written from the protocol description in 2026.
It shares no code with the Linux implementation.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Atsushi Furuta Aq Mt furuta@furuta.bsdclub.org
.Sh BUGS
Isochronous transfers are not implemented, so devices that depend on
them, such as audio interfaces and webcams, will not work.
.Pp
A control transfer that the USB stack splits across several submissions
cannot be expressed as a single USB/IP message and is rejected.
This does not arise during enumeration or for ordinary class drivers.
.Pp
The transfer interval is sent in milliseconds, which is what the
.Fx
USB stack works in, rather than in the frame units a Linux URB uses.
Interrupt endpoints work, but their polling rate may not be honoured
exactly.
